"""Class 10 SST Ch 20 Globalisation and the Indian Economy - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"MNCs set up their production units in countries where labour is cheap.", r"Low labour and other costs reduce the cost of production and help MNCs earn greater profits.", "a",
     r"Cheap labour lowers production cost and raises profit, which explains the MNCs' choice; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Tax on imports is a trade barrier.", r"The Indian government removed many barriers on foreign trade and foreign investment around 1991.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but R is a separate fact about liberalisation and does not explain why a tax is a barrier."),
 AR4(r"Globalisation has benefited all people in India equally.", r"Globalisation and greater competition have benefited consumers, but small producers and workers have often been hit by competition.", "d",
     r"A is false; the benefits have not been equal. R is true."),
 AR4(r"Foreign investment by MNCs is made to buy assets such as land, buildings and machines.", r"The most common route for MNC investment in a country is to set up new factories and never to buy local companies.", "c",
     r"A is true. R is false: the most common route is to buy up local companies and expand production."),
 AR4(r"Rapid improvement in technology has stimulated globalisation.", r"Developments in transport, and in information and communication technology, have made it easier and cheaper to spread production and services across countries.", "a",
     r"Cheaper and faster transport and communication made global production practical; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"The World Trade Organisation aims to liberalise international trade.", r"Trade in goods is the only way in which countries are connected today, as people do not move between countries at all.", "c",
     r"A is true. R is false: countries are also connected by investment, services and technology, and people do move in search of jobs and education, though with restrictions."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A large MNC designs its products in research centres in the United States and has the components manufactured in China. The components are shipped to Mexico and Eastern Europe, where the products are assembled and sold all over the world. Its customer care is carried out through call centres located in India. "
   r"(i) What is a multinational corporation? (ii) Why was China chosen for manufacturing components? (iii) Why is India chosen for customer care? (iv) How does this example show that production is spread across countries?",
   r"(i) A company that owns or controls production in more than one nation (1). (ii) It is a cheap manufacturing location (1). (iii) India has educated English-speaking youth and skilled engineers (1). (iv) The production process is divided into small parts, each placed in the country offering the best advantage in cost or skill or nearness to markets, so production is interlinked across the world (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Chinese manufacturers begin to export plastic toys to India. Within a year, 70 to 80 per cent of toy shops replace Indian toys with the cheaper Chinese ones. Buyers get more choice and lower prices, while Indian toy makers lose business. To protect them, the government considers putting a tax on imported toys. "
   r"(i) What is a tax on imports an example of? (ii) How did the Chinese toys benefit Indian buyers? (iii) What was the effect on Indian toy makers? (iv) What would happen to the price of imported toys if the tax is imposed?",
   r"(i) A trade barrier (1). (ii) Greater choice and lower prices (1). (iii) They faced losses, as their toys sold much less (1). (iv) The price paid by buyers would rise, so the toys would not be as cheap, imports would fall and Indian toy makers would gain (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Cargill Foods, a very large American MNC, has bought smaller Indian companies such as Parakh Foods. Parakh Foods had a large marketing network and four oil refineries. Cargill is now the largest producer of edible oil in India. In many industries, large MNCs also place orders with small producers and sell the goods under their own brand. "
   r"(i) Which Indian company was bought by Cargill Foods? (ii) How many oil refineries did that company have? (iii) What is the term for the money that MNCs spend on land, buildings and machines? (iv) How can large MNCs dominate small producers who supply them?",
   r"(i) Parakh Foods (1). (ii) Four (1). (iii) Investment (foreign investment) (1). (iv) MNCs have tremendous power to decide the price, quality, delivery and labour conditions for the small producers, who depend on their orders (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

MISTAKES = [
 r"Defining an MNC as any big company; an MNC is one that <b>owns or controls production in more than one nation</b>.",
 r"Mixing up <b>foreign trade</b> (buying and selling goods across borders) with <b>foreign investment</b> (money spent on assets by an MNC in another country).",
 r"Writing that liberalisation means <b>adding</b> trade barriers; it means <b>removing</b> barriers or restrictions, as India did from about 1991.",
 r"Saying globalisation benefited everyone equally; the chapter shows gains for consumers and some producers but stress for <b>small producers and workers</b>.",
 r"Listing only trade as the link between countries: also <b>investment, technology and the movement of people</b>.",
 r"Thinking that trade barriers are always bad or only developing countries use them: developed countries also kept them, and the WTO's rules are <b>not applied fairly</b>.",
]
